---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeSubnet from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeSubnet.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="cidr-to-ip-range">
  <DevEncodeSubnet client:load mode="cidr-range" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Expanding a block</h2>
    <p>
      A CIDR block is a starting address plus a prefix length. The prefix fixes the leading bits, and
      all combinations of the remaining bits are inside the block. The range always starts at the
      network address and ends at the broadcast address.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Block</th><th>First address</th><th>Last address</th><th>Count</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10.0.0.0/8</td><td>10.0.0.0</td><td>10.255.255.255</td><td>16,777,216</td></tr>
          <tr><td>172.16.0.0/12</td><td>172.16.0.0</td><td>172.31.255.255</td><td>1,048,576</td></tr>
          <tr><td>192.168.0.0/16</td><td>192.168.0.0</td><td>192.168.255.255</td><td>65,536</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100.64.0.0/10</td><td>100.64.0.0</td><td>100.127.255.255</td><td>4,194,304</td></tr>
          <tr><td>169.254.0.0/16</td><td>169.254.0.0</td><td>169.254.255.255</td><td>65,536</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      People often get 172.16.0.0/12 wrong. It ends at 172.31.255.255, not 172.16.255.255. With twelve
      fixed bits, the low nibble of the second octet is free, which gives 172.16 to 172.31.
    </p>

    <h2>Address range or host range?</h2>
    <p>
      The tool shows both, and they have different uses. A firewall rule, a route table entry or a
      security-group source needs the full address range, including the network and broadcast
      addresses. A DHCP pool or a static-allocation plan needs the host range, which excludes both. If
      you mix them up, the allocation can overlap the gateway.
    </p>

    <h2>Prefix lengths worth recognising</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>/32</code>: exactly one address. The usual way to name a single host in a rule.</li>
      <li><code>/31</code>: two addresses, both usable, for point-to-point links (RFC 3021).</li>
      <li><code>/30</code>: four addresses, two usable. The traditional point-to-point size.</li>
      <li><code>/24</code>: the classic &ldquo;one office LAN&rdquo;.</li>
      <li><code>/0</code>: everything. <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> is the default route, and in a security group it means the entire internet.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Dotted masks are accepted too</h2>
    <p>
      Older equipment, and Cisco configuration in particular, writes the full mask:
      <code>192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0</code>. Type <code>192.168.1.0/255.255.255.0</code> here and the
      tool converts it. It rejects a non-contiguous mask such as <code>255.0.255.0</code> with an
      explanation: a valid mask is always a run of ones followed by a run of zeros, so it can be written
      as a prefix length.
    </p>

    <h2>Wildcard masks are the other way round</h2>
    <p>
      Cisco access lists use a wildcard mask, which is the inverse: <code>0.0.0.255</code> where the
      subnet mask is <code>255.255.255.0</code>. A zero bit means &ldquo;must match&rdquo; and a one bit
      means &ldquo;don't care&rdquo;. The breakdown above shows the wildcard for your block, so you can
      paste it into an ACL.
    </p>

    <h2>Building allowlists</h2>
    <p>
      Vendors publish their egress addresses as CIDR blocks, and your firewall may need a range or an
      explicit list. Expand the block here to see how many addresses you will trust. Check this number
      before you approve a <code>/16</code> that looked small in the ticket.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
